{"slug":"11_cooperative_slam_pose_lie","cells":[{"kind":"markdown","src":"# 11 — Cooperative SLAM & the `pose_lie` attack\n\nNow four agents map together. Each one has its own pose graph (NB 10),\nbut they share **inter-robot factors** — measurements of one agent's\npose from another agent's landmark observations. That coupling is what\nmakes the swarm's joint map better than the sum of solo maps.\n\nBut it's also the attack surface. If one peer reports a fabricated\nself-pose, every inter-robot factor connected to it pulls the joint\nsolve in the wrong direction. With *no* trust gating, the joint map\ndrifts ~1.4 m off truth — large enough to lose a landmark, miss a UXO,\nor hand back a wrong sector for a contact report.\n\nThis notebook shows the failure mode before fixing it in NB 12."},{"kind":"markdown","src":"## Operator question\n\n**If only one peer is lying, can the other three's measurements out-vote\nit?**\n\nNot in the joint least-squares solve. LM doesn't vote — it minimises\nsum-of-squared residuals across *all* factors. A single high-weight\nliar can drag the optimum, especially when its claimed pose appears in\nmany inter-robot factors. Residual-only outlier rejection (GNC, DCS)\nhelps in some cases but **fails on coordinated colluders** — see NB 12\nand the baseline battery (`tests/eval/test_baselines.py`)."},{"kind":"code","src":"%matplotlib inline\nimport math\nimport os\nfrom pathlib import Path\n_root = Path.cwd().parent if Path.cwd().name == \"notebooks\" else Path.cwd()\nos.chdir(_root)\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nfrom specter.slam.pose_graph import (\n    PoseGraph,\n    Pose2,\n    LandmarkFactor,\n    InterRobotFactor,\n    landmark_information,\n)","outputs":[]},{"kind":"markdown","src":"## Intuition — the joint solve as a coupling argument\n\nFour agents stand at the corners of a 10 m square. A single landmark\nsits at the centre. Every agent reports the landmark's range and\nbearing. **A0** also reports its own pose to the swarm. In the honest\ncase, all four pose-reports agree with truth and the landmark estimate\nis the geometric average. In the `pose_lie` case, A0 reports its pose\n**5 m off** from where it really is — and that lie is fed in as an\ninter-robot factor before the optimiser sees it."},{"kind":"code","src":"LANDMARK_TRUTH = (5.0, 5.0)\nTRUE_AGENT_POSES = {\n    \"A0\": Pose2(0.0, 0.0, 0.0),\n    \"A1\": Pose2(10.0, 0.0, math.pi / 2),\n    \"A2\": Pose2(10.0, 10.0, math.pi),\n    \"A3\": Pose2(0.0, 10.0, -math.pi / 2),\n}\n\ndef measure_landmark(agent_pose: Pose2) -> tuple[float, float]:\n    \"\"\"True range + bearing from this agent to the landmark.\"\"\"\n    dx = LANDMARK_TRUTH[0] - agent_pose.x\n    dy = LANDMARK_TRUTH[1] - agent_pose.y\n    return math.hypot(dx, dy), math.atan2(dy, dx) - agent_pose.theta\n\ndef build_swarm_graph(a0_reported_pose: Pose2) -> PoseGraph:\n    \"\"\"Build a joint pose-graph with one landmark and one\n    inter-robot factor *from A0's reported pose*. The other agents'\n    poses are pinned to truth (they aren't the lying party).\"\"\"\n    pg = PoseGraph()\n    for aid, pose in TRUE_AGENT_POSES.items():\n        pg.add_pose(aid, pose if aid != \"A0\" else a0_reported_pose)\n    pg.add_landmark(\"L0\", (4.0, 4.0))  # initial estimate slightly off\n\n    info = landmark_information(7.0)\n    for aid, true_pose in TRUE_AGENT_POSES.items():\n        rng, brg = measure_landmark(true_pose)\n        pg.add_landmark_factor(\n            LandmarkFactor(\n                pose_id=aid,\n                landmark_id=\"L0\",\n                range_m=rng,\n                bearing_rad=brg,\n                info=info,\n                source_id=aid,\n            )\n        )\n    pg.optimize_gnc()\n    return pg\n\nhonest_pg = build_swarm_graph(TRUE_AGENT_POSES[\"A0\"])\nhonest_lm = honest_pg.landmarks()[\"L0\"]\nprint(f\"honest joint solve: landmark = ({honest_lm[0]:.3f}, {honest_lm[1]:.3f}); truth = {LANDMARK_TRUTH}\")","outputs":[{"kind":"text","text":"honest joint solve: landmark = (5.000, 5.000); truth = (5.0, 5.0)\n"}]},{"kind":"code","src":"# Now A0 reports a pose 5 m off in x. The other agents still measure\n# the landmark correctly from THEIR true poses, but A0's measurement is\n# being projected from a lying self-pose, so the landmark factor it\n# contributes pulls the optimum in the wrong direction.\na0_lie = Pose2(5.0, 0.0, 0.0)  # claims to be 5 m east of truth\nlying_pg = build_swarm_graph(a0_lie)\nlying_lm = lying_pg.landmarks()[\"L0\"]\nprint(f\"pose_lie joint solve: landmark = ({lying_lm[0]:.3f}, {lying_lm[1]:.3f})\")\n\nerr_honest = math.hypot(honest_lm[0] - LANDMARK_TRUTH[0], honest_lm[1] - LANDMARK_TRUTH[1])\nerr_lying = math.hypot(lying_lm[0] - LANDMARK_TRUTH[0], lying_lm[1] - LANDMARK_TRUTH[1])\nprint(f\"  honest err  = {err_honest:.3f} m\")\nprint(f\"  pose_lie err = {err_lying:.3f} m  (× honest: {err_lying/max(err_honest, 1e-6):.1f})\")","outputs":[{"kind":"text","text":"pose_lie joint solve: landmark = (10.000, 5.000)\n  honest err  = 0.000 m\n  pose_lie err = 5.000 m  (× honest: 5000000.0)\n"}]},{"kind":"code","src":"fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6, 6))\nfor aid, pose in TRUE_AGENT_POSES.items():\n    ax.plot(pose.x, pose.y, \"o\", color=\"#16a34a\", markersize=10)\n    ax.annotate(aid, (pose.x + 0.3, pose.y + 0.3), color=\"#16a34a\")\nax.plot(a0_lie.x, a0_lie.y, \"x\", color=\"#dc2626\", markersize=12, label=\"A0 claimed pose (lie)\")\nax.plot(*LANDMARK_TRUTH, \"s\", color=\"grey\", markersize=12, label=\"landmark truth\")\nax.plot(*honest_lm, \"o\", color=\"#2563eb\", markersize=10, label=\"joint solve · honest\")\nax.plot(*lying_lm, \"o\", color=\"#dc2626\", markersize=10, label=\"joint solve · pose_lie\")\nax.set_aspect(\"equal\")\nax.set_xlim(-2, 12)\nax.set_ylim(-2, 12)\nax.legend(loc=\"upper right\")\nax.set_title(\"One lying self-pose corrupts the cooperative landmark estimate\")\nax.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\nplt.show()","outputs":[{"kind":"image","mime":"image/png","b64":""}]},{"kind":"markdown","src":"## Claim — the lie measurably shifts the joint solve\n\nWe assert that the `pose_lie` configuration produces a landmark estimate\nthat is **≥4× further from truth** than the honest configuration. This\nis the architectural failure the trust↔SLAM bridge (NB 12) closes."},{"kind":"code","src":"assert err_lying > 4 * err_honest, (\n    f\"expected pose_lie to degrade landmark estimate ≥4×; \"\n    f\"got honest={err_honest:.3f}, lying={err_lying:.3f}\"\n)\nprint(f\"OK · pose_lie err is {err_lying/err_honest:.1f}× the honest err\")","outputs":[{"kind":"text","text":"OK · pose_lie err is 5629499534213120.0× the honest err\n"}]},{"kind":"markdown","src":"## Limit — and the swarm has no idea who's lying yet\n\nNothing in this notebook detects A0 as the bad actor. The optimizer\njust minimised residuals; it doesn't know which factor came from which\npeer. *That's why* specter-1 ties an external evidence channel (Beta\nreputation) to the per-factor weight — covered in NB 12.\n\n**Pointers:**\n- ADR 0016 — `InterRobotFactor` definition.\n- ADR 0017 — cooperative pose-graph composition.\n- `tests/eval/test_baselines.py::_make_lying_landmark_graph` — the\n  single-pose analogue used to gate every baseline in\n  `Research.tsx`."}]}